{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Expansion.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Expansion.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"polynomial expansion polynomial expansion","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Expansion.md","fileName":"Polynomial Expansion.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Polynomial Expansion","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Polynomial and Rational Expressions]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Polynomial and Rational Expressions","Distributive Property","Like Terms","Polynomial Factorization","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Expansion.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Expansion.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"polynomial expansion polynomial expansion","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Expansion.md","fileName":"Polynomial Expansion.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Polynomial Expansion","heading":"Polynomial Expansion","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Polynomial expansion removes products and grouping symbols by applying the [[Distributive Property]]. Every term in one factor multiplies every term in the other factors, after which [[Like Terms]] are combined.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$(2x+3)(5x+7)=10x^2+14x+15x+21=10x^2+29x+21.$$\n\nExpansion and [[Polynomial Factorization]] are reverse views of the same equality. Expansion exposes coefficients and like terms, while factorization exposes multiplicative structure and possible zeros.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Polynomial and Rational Expressions","Distributive Property","Like Terms","Polynomial Factorization","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Polynomial Expansion.md::2","sourceKey":"6 -